00:00:05.749 --> 00:00:07.730 align:start position:0%
an asteroid hurtling towards Earth on  course to smash into the surface with  the force of a nuclear bomb it's a scary  prospect thankfully asteroid impacts of  this magnitude a rare but they do happen  and worryingly they can come without  warning in February of 2013 that's  exactly what happened near a small town  called Chell event in Russia nine months  on and scientists are starting to piece  together the details of this  once-in-a-lifetime event the answers  have come from an unlikely place YouTube  the Chelyabinsk asteroid was filmed from  hundreds of angles by hundreds of  cameras mobile phones security cameras  or dash cams small dashboard mounted  video cameras designed to tackle car  crime hiding inside these videos is a  wealth of information and that's helped  scientists build the most complete  picture of an asteroid impact to date so  what can you learn from a YouTube video  well researchers recreated the amateur  video shots but at night this allowed  them to use the Stars to pinpoint the  asteroids trajectory when the asteroid  hit the Earth's atmosphere it was  traveling at around 19 kilometres a  second at such massive speeds the drag  caused it to heat up eventually  exploding into a fireball and by  measuring the brightness of this  fireball the researchers estimated its  energy the explosion created a pressure  wave which hurtled towards earth  shattering thousands of windows and by  tracking the movement of shards of glass  as the window smashed scientists could  calculate the force of the pressure wave  all of this helped scientists work out  the Chelyabinsk asteroid was similar in  size to a six story building and the  fireball explosion was about seven times  larger than the nuclear bomb that  destroyed Hiroshima such huge explosions  are so rare that up until now scientists  have had to rely on models designed to  simulate nuclear bombs to understand  fireballs but the data gathered about  Chelyabinsk has allowed them to build  much more accurate models these suggests  that even large fireballs such as the  one over Chelyabinsk may cause less  damage than we'd expect unlike a bomb  which releases all of its energy from a  single point the fireballs energy is  spread out as it moves across the sky it  could be a century before the next  asteroid of this magnitude comes along  but when it does make sure to grab your  camera phone because scientists are  going to want to see it
 

